Since you already have 5 years of Laravel/PHP experience, the best Node.js/Express project for interviews is one that shows you understand real backend architecture, not just CRUD. You want something that demonstrates:

Authentication + authorization
REST APIs (or GraphQL optional)
Database design
Middleware usage
File uploads / background jobs
Clean architecture (controllers/services/repositories)
Real-world features (like notifications, caching, etc.)

Here’s a strong interview-grade project idea:

🚀 Project Idea: “Smart Task & Team Management System (Like Mini Jira / Trello)”
🧠 Why this project is powerful

It mimics real SaaS tools used in companies. If you build this well, you can confidently say:

“I built a production-style backend similar to Jira/Trello with role-based teams, task workflows, and real-time updates.”

🏗️ Core Features (MVP)
1. Authentication System
Register / Login
JWT-based auth (access + refresh tokens)
Password hashing (bcrypt)
Forgot password (email reset link)

👉 Skills shown:

Security
Token handling
Middleware
2. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)

Roles:

Admin (team owner)
Manager
Member

Permissions:

Admin: manage team, delete tasks, assign roles
Manager: assign tasks, create projects
Member: update task status only

👉 This is VERY important for interviews.

3. Projects Module
Create project
Update project
Delete project
Assign team members to project

Each project has:

name
description
owner
members
4. Task Management System

Each task includes:

title
description
status: todo | in-progress | done
priority: low / medium / high
assigned user
due date

Features:

Create task
Update task status (Kanban style)
Assign task to user
Filter tasks by status/user/project
5. Comments on Tasks
Add comment
Edit comment
Delete comment
Show comment history

👉 Shows relational DB skills

6. Notifications System (Very impressive)

When:

task assigned → notify user
task updated → notify project owner

Options:

Email (Nodemailer)
OR in-app notifications table
7. File Uploads
Attach files to tasks (images, PDFs)
Use:
Multer (Node.js middleware)
or AWS S3 (bonus)
⚙️ Advanced Features (to stand out in interviews)
8. Real-time updates (BIG BOOST 🚀)

Use:

Socket.io

Example:

When a task moves to “Done”, team sees it instantly
9. Redis caching (optional but strong)
Cache project/task lists
Improve performance
10. Audit Log System (VERY IMPRESSIVE)

Track:

who changed task
what changed
when it changed

Example:

John changed task status from "todo" to "done"
11. API Rate limiting & security
express-rate-limit
helmet
cors config
🗄️ Suggested Database Design (MongoDB or MySQL)

Tables/Collections:

Users
id
name
email
password
role
Projects
id
name
description
owner_id
Tasks
id
project_id
assigned_to
title
status
priority
Comments
id
task_id
user_id
comment
Notifications
id
user_id
message
read_status
🧱 Tech Stack (Recommended)
Node.js
Express.js
MongoDB (Mongoose) OR MySQL (Sequelize/Prisma)
JWT authentication
Socket.io
Multer / AWS S3
Nodemailer
Redis (optional)
📁 Folder Structure (important for interviews)
src/
  config/
  controllers/
  services/
  models/
  routes/
  middlewares/
  utils/
  jobs/
  sockets/

👉 If you structure like this, interviewers get impressed instantly.

🎯 Bonus: How to present it in interview

You can say:

“I built a role-based project management system similar to Jira with authentication, project-task workflow, real-time updates using WebSockets, and audit logging for all changes. I used Express.js with layered architecture to keep business logic separate from controllers.”

⭐ If you want even better ideas (alternatives)

If you don’t like this one, here are 2 other strong options:

1. E-commerce backend (Amazon-like)
Cart system
Payment integration (Stripe)
Order tracking
Inventory system
2. Job portal system (LinkedIn-lite)
Companies post jobs
Users apply
Resume upload
Admin dashboard
